    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that all sounds a bit vague though. like, when they say it's an \"investment,\" does that actually mean anything for a town in the midwest where the main employer might have to cut staff to pay for these tech upgrades? it feels like their arguments are just based on these big abstract ideas rather than the actual math of running a plant. do those arguments actually hold up when you look at the real-world economic trade-offs?",
